WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:10.410
Belgrade wakes us up, and Belgrade has significant artists it can be proud of.

2
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:15.210
We are a people who have always managed, even in difficult moments,

3
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.390
to find a way for artistic expression, for creativity,

4
00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:23.790
and if we can boast of any good traits, then this is certainly one of them.

5
00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:30.250
When you mark 30 years of work, and you're not that old,

6
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.950
at least by today's standards, it's a great success.

7
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:41.530
Besides, if behind you there are big awards and projects, you really have something to boast about.

8
00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.930
With us is Slavimir Stojanović, first of all a graphic designer,

9
00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:49.290
and what else he did and how during these past 30 years, we'll see in the coming minutes.

10
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:49.810
Welcome.

11
00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:50.850
Glad to be with you.

12
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.670
Are you a bit embarrassed when you're introduced?

13
00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.790
Well yes, when they say I've been working for 30 years — yes.

14
00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.050
But I started early, indeed, I was lucky to win

15
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:06.570
an open poster competition in '86, actually in '85.

16
00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:07.870
That means at 16, right?

17
00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:12.970
Yes, I was 16, and that's when I got the shot for this work,

18
00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:15.110
and even got my first fee then.

19
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.290
With that money, I remember, I bought sneakers, that's how much I could allot myself.

20
00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:24.870
But I continued, just kept working.

21
00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.430
Literally the next year I got some award again, and so on.

22
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:33.590
I simply got hooked on these competitions and began entering non-stop.

23
00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.070
That is, while still a schoolboy — I attended the secondary design school.

24
00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:41.810
I was actually very lucky to understand in time what I was interested in.

25
00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.030
That was precisely my next question.

26
00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:49.450
Actually, how did you discover art, and how, so you say, very early,

27
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:55.790
while usually kids, young people, dash from faculty to faculty until they finally find themselves.

28
00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.750
Well, it was, I must admit, a completely different time.

29
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:02.110
When I was growing up, in the seventies and eighties,

30
00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.550
sport was important at the time, as it is today.

31
00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.530
More or less, but important.

32
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:12.130
We followed the results, people from sport were important people, just like today.

33
00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.090
But people from culture were incomparably more important.

34
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.990
And I looked at those role models.

35
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:23.090
I did sports and to this day I play football three times a week.

36
00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.810
So I haven't given that up.

37
00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.950
I thought I'd be an athlete, a footballer or something like that.

38
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:33.490
However, I learned to draw and saw that this has a far greater

39
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:38.710
social effect — the attention of the opposite sex and of friends.

40
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:39.990
At that moment that was very important.

41
00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:46.170
Yes, it was important, and then I realized that some level of communication was possible here,

42
00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.570
but above all through entertainment.

43
00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.350
I started as an illustrator, I drew caricatures

44
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:54.190
and that way earned points in the group.

45
00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.970
How did you come to graphic design?

46
00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:02.410
It's a field that is highly valued and well paid today.

47
00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:03.470
And how did you discover it?

48
00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:08.350
Well, in the secondary design school I saw that there was a place for me here.

49
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:14.590
Because I always wanted to say as much as possible with the simplest possible forms, elements,

50
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.510
and for it to be, God willing, witty at the same time.

51
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.850
And somehow graphic design was for me... it came easiest to me.

52
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.190
And that's something worth advising everyone.

53
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:28.050
What comes easiest to you, that's what you should do all your life.

54
00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.890
That is, provided you enjoy it.

55
00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.030
I discovered this.

56
00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.110
And second — a technological revolution happened.

57
00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:39.390
At the end of the eighties the first computers started to appear

58
00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.970
that you could have at home.

59
00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:46.770
And I was lucky that I again ended up at a studio of friends

60
00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:51.010
who were the first in the area to have such a computer, and I quickly began working on it.

61
00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:55.670
And I saw that I reach a result much faster than when I sit all night drawing comics.

62
00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.450
My neck hurt, I had spondylosis at 16–17.

63
00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:00.450
I mean, like an old man.

64
00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:05.670
And it was really agonizing for me to sit at the computer so much,

65
00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.690
because I loved sports very much.

66
00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:12.690
So thanks to design I had much more time.

67
00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.150
In fact it was laziness that gave me the push.

68
00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:19.950
Laziness is sometimes useful too.

69
00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:25.450
In the sense of sitting at a computer in one place — I do that very rarely.

70
00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:30.810
In fact I think everything up in my head while doing anything else.

71
00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.630
Whether I'm walking in nature, playing with my daughter or whatever.

72
00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:38.510
During free activities I'm much more creative than

73
00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:42.310
when you sit me down: sit now, you have half an hour, go on, work.

74
00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.710
You say it's very important for a person to do what they love, to enjoy it.

75
00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:51.270
What happened to us? Many people choose some other occupations.

76
00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.490
It seems everything is simple. You follow what your heart tells you,

77
00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:59.070
what your soul tells you. And on the other hand, we have a large number of people

78
00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:02.310
who are dissatisfied precisely because they didn't take the right path,

79
00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:03.070
the one they loved.

80
00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:07.850
Well yes. We live in a society... partly it's a global problem too,

81
00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.330
but we seem to live in a society of broken mirrors.

82
00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.930
People don't know how to look at themselves properly

83
00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.790
or don't know how to see themselves from the right angle.

84
00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.250
It's a privilege of only a few people who end up being happy,

85
00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.910
that their mirror works, that they have a working mirror at home,

86
00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.790
in which they regularly look.

87
00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.570
With us people see themselves poorly,

88
00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.290
and there are very many people who are unhappy because of this,

89
00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:33.870
because they think they're owed something.

90
00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:38.070
Much more, and better, and bigger, and more expensive, and so on.

91
00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.250
And in fact all of them are victims

92
00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:45.050
of the bombardment from this industry of desire production.

93
00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:47.810
It's advertising, in which I've for years

94
00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.690
persistently worked for various other brands.

95
00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:52.250
And do you feel good there?

96
00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:56.030
Well, it's, how to put it, a double-edged sword,

97
00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.390
which is why I do my own authored works

98
00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:01.470
and exhibitions that criticize this value system,

99
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.430
where I, as a person from the inside, have the right

100
00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.150
to try to fix it from within.

101
00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:08.870
And as much as it works out, so much it works out.

102
00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.970
I mean, it's important that someone from the inside

103
00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.590
draw attention to the shortcomings.

104
00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.910
That was my role.

105
00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:19.250
I've now not been working in advertising for some time,

106
00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.770
I do graphic design more,

107
00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.490
precisely for these moral reasons

108
00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:27.570
and because our advertising in Serbia

109
00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:30.730
comes down to bare sales, and nothing more.

110
00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.610
There's simply no room for any first-class creativity.

111
00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:35.590
Very rarely can you see

112
00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.090
fantastic advertising on television.

113
00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:40.790
And how did you react,

114
00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:42.130
while you were in that world,

115
00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:43.310
to certain instructions,

116
00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:44.630
when someone tells you:

117
00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:46.510
this is how it should look?

118
00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:47.250
Terrible, terrible.

119
00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.330
It got to the point that in the middle of a meeting

120
00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:50.350
I would simply get up and leave.

121
00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:52.890
But it's important — sometimes to say "no." — Very.

122
00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:00.030
That is, a person has to settle this with himself.

123
00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.970
So my mirror broke, more than once

124
00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:04.850
was sent for repair.

125
00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:08.050
Excellent. Couldn't be better.

126
00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.430
What will visitors, your friends,

127
00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.350
admirers of your work on September 3

128
00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.070
see at the Museum of Applied Art,

129
00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:17.470
and afterwards, while the exhibition runs?

130
00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:21.010
Well, they'll see more than 500 works.

131
00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:24.090
From larger to smaller.

132
00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:27.550
But there, basically, is this whole range

133
00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:28.920
of activities, from illustration,

134
00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.910
when at first my models were

135
00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:34.610
Mirko Ilić, Duško Petričić,

136
00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.490
Jugoslav Vlahović, these masters

137
00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:39.810
of our local illustration and caricature.

138
00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:42.230
And then their imprint is visible

139
00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:43.110
on some works too.

140
00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:46.170
Later — the influence of world trends.

141
00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.910
Until I found some language of my own.

142
00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:51.550
The exhibition is called "Complex Simplicity."

143
00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:54.070
That is, it's a kind of creative maxim

144
00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:54.730
that I adhere to.

145
00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:58.110
To say as much as possible with as few elements as possible.

146
00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:01.710
That is, minimalism that contains much more

147
00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:03.190
than it seems at first glance.

148
00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:06.200
And, of course, all spheres of work with communications,

149
00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.750
such as CD covers,

150
00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:11.950
from Rambo Amadeus to EKV,

151
00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.850
"Partibrejkers," everything I did.

152
00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:17.190
And there are many logos for big and less

153
00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:20.890
and less important companies and magazines,

154
00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:22.510
New Moment, which I did.

155
00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:27.470
All sorts, literally everything I touched

156
00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:29.170
in the field of culture.

157
00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:31.370
There are even those award-winning works

158
00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:32.710
from the field of advertising,

159
00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:34.930
where I was given room to maneuver.

160
00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:38.450
And, of course, that's two thirds of the exhibition,

161
00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:40.170
applied art,

162
00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:42.370
and one third is devoted

163
00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:43.850
to my brand Futro,

164
00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.830
through which I try,

165
00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.460
again, with some humor to entertain

166
00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:50.670
and to make people want to buy

167
00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.410
T-shirts, bags or whatever I have

168
00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:53.050
in the range.

169
00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.310
How can young designers, artists

170
00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.030
get onto some path,

171
00:08:58.150 --> 00:08:59.550
how to earn in Serbia?

172
00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:01.710
What to follow, what not to follow?

173
00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:03.610
First, this shouldn't be the main imperative

174
00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:04.230
at all.

175
00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:05.930
It shouldn't, ever.

176
00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:08.310
It shouldn't be a motive

177
00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.170
for any creative work.

178
00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:12.370
When from within

179
00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.270
you feel strength,

180
00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.490
then you need to release this strength

181
00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.870
where it makes sense to release it.

182
00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.670
First you need to grow stronger from within.

183
00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:21.870
This is the biggest problem.

184
00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:24.630
School, the academy should serve for this,

185
00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.350
society should give a push

186
00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:30.130
to talented young people and so on.

187
00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:32.150
We have various cases here now.

188
00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.510
Although now, I must admit,

189
00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:36.350
as a society we have formed

190
00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:38.270
into a sports-and-recreation society,

191
00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:40.050
not a cultural society.

192
00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:42.050
And this is a big misfortune for all people who

193
00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.730
work on some artistic statement,

194
00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:46.330
because they're somehow pushed

195
00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:47.650
aside.

196
00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:49.870
I can't complain, I even have more

197
00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.050
than I need

198
00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.110
of some presence in the media.

199
00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:56.230
But still, it's always good

200
00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:58.370
to get awards at festivals

201
00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:00.050
and publish that in newspapers,

202
00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:04.070
and not just some records in sports.

203
00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.010
How much in life do we manage

204
00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:07.630
to complicate the simple?

205
00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:10.330
Or simply to complicate?

206
00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.150
This is, basically, a life ambition.

207
00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:14.250
To try to live

208
00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:16.150
as simply as possible.

209
00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:19.010
Because life is exceptionally complex.

210
00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.670
Above all it's a search for

211
00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:22.870
one's own identity,

212
00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:24.950
this is the biggest challenge today.

213
00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:26.830
Because the global crisis

214
00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.970
of human civilization is a crisis

215
00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:30.890
of identity, because

216
00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:32.730
it has become clear that the material

217
00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:34.230
cannot be more important

218
00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:36.730
than the spiritual. I'm not speaking in a religious sense,

219
00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:38.550
but literally about the inner world, which is

220
00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:40.650
connected with the universe

221
00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:43.030
and with these truly noble things.

222
00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:44.110
This is what I

223
00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:46.410
really strive for. That's why everything of mine is so

224
00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.770
steeped in humor,

225
00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:50.590
so that, basically, people with a smile

226
00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:52.870
on their faces consume art,

227
00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:55.050
and not so that we

228
00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:55.530
all somehow suffer.

229
00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:58.750
Let's end this conversation with one more

230
00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:00.630
question. What are the challenges for you today

231
00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:02.130
in art,

232
00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:04.310
in design? What haven't you

233
00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:05.530
accomplished, but would like to?

234
00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:24.070
To force myself.

235
00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:27.270
We're all somehow attuned here

236
00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.250
to this inner perpetual motion machine,

237
00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:30.510
where we have to inspire

238
00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:32.770
ourselves.

239
00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:35.650
Objectively, really, to be honest,

240
00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:37.350
this city, specifically,

241
00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:38.690
where I was born,

242
00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.110
doesn't inspire me at all.

243
00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:42.850
Except, of course, certain people to whom

244
00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:45.410
I can't wait to show what I've done,

245
00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:47.290
but the environment — what it is, how much it has,

246
00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:49.410
so to speak, been polluted

247
00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:50.730
with everything it shouldn't be —

248
00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:53.250
isn't suitable for the flourishing

249
00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:54.950
of any talent whatsoever.

250
00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:56.710
There's no systemic

251
00:11:57.970 --> 00:11:59.090
support for the beautiful,

252
00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:01.210
for quality. We see this

253
00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:02.810
wherever we turn around.

254
00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:04.730
Of course, Belgrade looks better than

255
00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:06.390
in the nineties. That's all

256
00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:08.470
anyone can say.

257
00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:11.630
When it was terrible, it can also be less terrible.

258
00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:12.310
So

259
00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:14.530
for design and aesthetics

260
00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:16.930
there's still room

261
00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:18.050
to do something here.

262
00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.470
Thank you very much for your time, and here's to

